[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Title card "EXTRACTION" over a building exterior.
AUDIO: 'The quick start video covered the basic workflow. In this one, I\'ll go through the settings in more detail. Extraction. You can trim the in-n-out points of each clip.'

[00:12] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows "PINHOLE SPLITS" settings with options for 1, 5, or 9 splits, detailing coverage, view angles, and speed.
AUDIO: 'Select how often frames are extracted, either in seconds or in frames. When pick sharpest frame is on, the surrounding window of frames is compared and the sharpest one is kept. Pinhole splits. One split covers about'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'half of the fisheye. one undistorted frontal view per lens at 115°. It doesn\'t cover the whole fisheye view. Five splits gives full fisheye coverage, frontal plus up, down, left, and right at 95°. There\'s a dedicated frontal tile'

[00:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI highlights the "9 splits 90°" option for pinhole splits, showing its full fisheye coverage and densest overlap.
AUDIO: 'for the sharpest sampling. This is the best workflow default. Nine splits also covers the full fisheye at 90° per view. It has the densest overlap between tiles and the most images, the slowest option.'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '3D GU mode is only available with fisheye input and lickedfeld studio. It trains directly on the fisheye images, so the pinhole stage is skipped. Turning this on also selects the licked feld back end in the train tab. Stitched 360'

[01:01] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI displays a file selection dialog within 360° Gaussian Pro, showing various video files and a preview of a 360-degree video.
AUDIO: 'equangular input is always split into six cube map faces. With add batch input, you can add multiple videos to one project. Trim, extraction, and masking can be'

[01:16] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the "Masking" section with "Circle Mask" selected, displaying options for presets (Insta360 X5, DJI Osmo 360, Antigravity A1) and a size slider.
AUDIO: 'changed for each clip individually. Masking The circle mask is needed with fisheye'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inputs. It\'s a round mask around the image edge that hides the soft fisheye rim. Presets for common rigs are detected automatically, and the size slider grows or shrinks the mask. Keyword masking uses SAM 3 to mask'

[01:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows "Keyword SAM3" selected in the Masking section, with a text input for prompts like "person, phone" and a detection threshold slider.
AUDIO: 'elements with a text prompt. Add your keywords and try them on a single frame with detect this frame before you mask the whole clip. The detection threshold sets how'

[01:57] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the "Paintbrush" tool selected in the Masking section, with options to paint on the current frame or all frames, and to paint on both cameras.
AUDIO: 'confident SAM 3 must be before a match is masked. If elements are not detected, lower the threshold. It then catches weak and partial matches, too. If unwanted elements are detected, raise it so only clear matches are masked. With the paintbrush, you can paint a mask'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manually. Paint on one frame only or apply it to all frames. And you can paint on both lenses at the same time. alignment.'

[02:34] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI displays the "Alignment" tab in 360° Gaussian Pro, showing various settings for rotation, translation, scale, point size, and options like "Matcher," "Overlap," and "Loop detection."
AUDIO: 'The gold info button opens a quick guide with the recommended setting combinations. Alignment runs in two stages. Stage one runs coal map structure from motion on the raw fisheye frames to recover every'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera pose. Stage two renders pinhole tiles from those poses and re-triangulates a much denser cloud. With reuse fisheye features turned on, the pipeline reuses the features it already found during alignment instead'

[02:59] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI focuses on the "Matcher" setting within the Alignment tab, showing "Sequential" as the default option.
AUDIO: 'of detecting new ones on each pinhole view. That makes the densification steps several times faster. The trade-off is that fine details can come out a little softer. So, reach for this when you want a quick result and leave it off when you'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want maximum quality. The default matcher is sequential yet which works best with video. Exhaustive compares every pair, the most thorough but slow on long clips. Spatial uses GPS neighbors and is picked'

[03:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An explanatory graphic illustrates "SEQUENTIAL OVERLAP," showing how frames are compared with neighbors on each side, with different overlap values for raw fisheye and stitched 360.
AUDIO: 'automatically when a same name.GPX file sits next to the clip. The overlap setting is simply how many neighbors that is on each side. It picks which frames get compared. Raw fisheye defaults to a wider window, 17, because'

[03:36] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An explanatory graphic illustrates "LOOP DETECTION," showing a closed loop of frames and indicating drift correction.
AUDIO: 'each lens sees only half the world. A stitched 360 frame sees everything, so 10 is plenty. If you get multiple separate alignments, raise the overlap. If the same features appear twice, turn'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'glow map off. Loop detection also matches frames far apart in time. So when the walk returns to a place it is seen before, the drift is corrected. Lens prior give Coal Map known focal'

[04:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the "GLOMAP global mapper" option in the Alignment tab, with a tooltip explaining its function for solving all camera poses at once.
AUDIO: 'length and distortion numbers before matching starts. On auto, the first successful alignment calibrates your camera\'s lenses automatically, and every run after that reuses your camera\'s own numbers for faster, more reliable'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'alignment. Glow Map solves all camera poses at once instead of registering images one by one. It\'s much faster in minutes instead of tens of minutes. If the model comes out bent or misaligned, turn it off to use the incremental'

[04:25] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the "Geometry Timeline Check" option in the Alignment tab, with a tooltip describing its experimental nature for dual fisheye.
AUDIO: 'mapper. Geometry timeline check is experimental and for dual fisheye only. It checks that your walkthrough doesn\'t break physics. Frames far apart in time shouldn\'t be in the same place and repairs the model when the same content in two different rooms gets folded'

[04:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the "Preset quality" dropdown in the Alignment tab, with "Default" selected.
AUDIO: 'together. Preset quality is one dial for quality against speed in VRAM. Low quality uses fewer features and smaller GPU buffers for 4 GB cards and quick previews. Default is the coal map defaults and good for most clips. High'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality uses stricter features and guided matching. A denser, more accurate cloud, but slower. With add detail photos, you can register extra photos from a DSLR, drone, or phone into the aligned model. To sharpen important'

[05:04] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI displays the "Advanced settings" section, showing command-line interface (CLI) flags for feature_extractor, matcher, bundle_adjuster, and cleaning stages.
AUDIO: 'areas, each camera goes in its own subfolder, JPG or PNG only. Add detail photos before running the pipeline. Advanced settings opens the CLI drawer with one tap per pipeline phase and every flag has its own tool tip.'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Editing anything flips the preset to custom. No alignment skips coal map for this clip, but still extracts, masks, and'

[05:30] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows a 3D viewport displaying a sparse point cloud model, with controls for rotation, translation, scale, and point size.
AUDIO: 'renders the pinhole split into 00 train data. So you can align the tiles in another application. When alignment is done, the sparse model is visualized in the 3D preview. Middle drag rotates. Right drag pans and the'

[05:42] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows an image viewer displaying a single pinhole image extracted from the 360-degree footage, with navigation controls.
AUDIO: 'wheel zooms. Left click selects a camera and left drag rubber bands several of them. Delete excludes the selected cameras and cotlr plus z undo it. Align ground to grid rotates the model so the floor lies flat on the grid. Double'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click an image in the list to inspect it and paint masks. The edits are non-destructive or click apply edits to bake them into the sparse folder the train uses. The log shows the process in detail,'

[06:08] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows a file explorer window displaying the folder structure of extracted pinhole images, organized by camera and frame.
AUDIO: 'including the settings used and how long each step took. Next to the input video, a folder with the same name is created, containing all the outputs. Zero train data contains the images, masks, and sparse folders.'

[06:22] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The UI shows the 360° Gaussian Pro interface with the 3D sparse model viewer open, and a file explorer window overlaid, demonstrating the integration of outputs.
AUDIO: 'Drag and drop this folder into licked studio to start training manually. You can keep all the files or for example, delete everything except zero soul train data and training us output. If you keep all the files, you can drag'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and drop the same video clip into the program later and the whole process is preserved. This way, for example, different alignment settings are faster to test it. That\'s all the features. The full documentation is on the website and'

